+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report for `lz4_flex`
+
+Added with `hegeltest` v0.28.2 (library crate name `hegel`). All new tests live in the
+existing test files: `tests/tests.rs` (integration properties) and the `#[cfg(test)]`
+modules of `src/fastcpy.rs` / `src/fastcpy_unsafe.rs` (ports of the pre-existing
+proptest PBTs). The pre-existing `proptest` suite (3 properties) was fully ported and
+the `proptest` dev-dependency removed.
+
+Shared generators: `payload()` (mixes incompressible random segments with repeated
+patterns, empty up to a few hundred KB, crossing the 64KB hash-table switch and the
+128KB linked-mode ext-dict handoff) and `frame_config()` (arbitrary `FrameInfo`
+configurations rather than canned ones — the "generate configurations, not just
+payloads" pattern; the crate's own `fuzz/` targets only ever use 4 of the 6 block
+sizes and never generate `content_size` or `legacy_frame`).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| # | Property | Test | Evidence |
+|---|----------|------|----------|
+| 1 | Roundtrip: block raw, block size-prepended, frame (both block modes), cross-checked against C++ lz4 (`lzzzz`) | `hegel_roundtrip_block_and_frame` (`tests/tests.rs`) | Port of the old `proptest_roundtrip`; crate docs' roundtrip examples; existing `test_roundtrip` oracle |
+| 2 | `compress_prepend_size` output == LE-u32 length ++ `compress` output, and `uncompressed_size` reads it back | `hegel_prepend_size_consistency` | Doc comments on `compress_prepend_size` and `uncompressed_size` |
+| 3 | Dictionary roundtrip: `compress_prepend_size_with_dict` / `decompress_size_prepended_with_dict` with the same dict (dict sizes cross MINMATCH=4 fallback and the 64KB window truncation; sometimes dict==data to force ext-dict matches) | `hegel_roundtrip_with_dict` | Doc comments pairing the two functions; `init_dict` truncation code in `src/block/compress.rs` |
+| 4 | A buffer of exactly `get_maximum_output_size(len)` is sufficient for `compress_into` | `hegel_compress_into_max_output_size_is_sufficient` | Doc comments on `get_maximum_output_size` and `compress_into` |
+| 5 | A reused `CompressTable` roundtrips for every input in a sequence (incl. the transparent Small→Large upgrade at 64KB); no stale state leaks between inputs | `hegel_compress_table_reuse_roundtrips` | `CompressTable` docs ("pass it ... repeatedly", "transparently upgraded"); stale-state bug pattern |
+| 6 | `decompress` accepts any `min_uncompressed_size` >= actual size and returns the same data | `hegel_decompress_accepts_larger_size_hint` | Doc: "needs to be equal or larger than the uncompressed size" |
+| 7 | Frame roundtrip holds for every configuration expressible via public `FrameInfo` (all 6 `BlockSize` variants, both `BlockMode`s, both checksums, optional `content_size`, `legacy_frame` flag) | `hegel_frame_roundtrip_any_config` | Public API surface of `FrameInfo`/`BlockSize`; fuzzer `fuzz_roundtrip_frame.rs` covers only a 4-size subset |
+| 8 | Write chunking invariance: arbitrary `write` splits + arbitrary intermediate `flush`es decompress to the same data as one `write_all` | `hegel_frame_write_chunking_invariance` | `FrameEncoder` doc: "Writes are buffered automatically"; `io::Write` contract |
+| 9 | Read chunking invariance: `FrameDecoder` with arbitrary small read-buffer sizes produces the same bytes as `read_to_end` | `hegel_frame_read_chunking_invariance` | `io::Read` contract; generalizes fixed-chunk reads in `fuzz_roundtrip_frame.rs` |
+| 10 | Concatenated frames: payloads written through one encoder via `try_finish` are read back one frame per `read_to_end` call | `hegel_frame_concatenated_frames` | Generalizes the existing `concatenated` unit test |
+| 11 | Corruption robustness: with block+content checksums enabled, flipping any single byte of a frame never panics the decoder and is never silently accepted with different output | `hegel_frame_corruption_never_panics_or_misdecodes` | Generalizes the existing `checksums` unit test (which corrupts two fixed positions) |
+| 12 | `fastcpy::slice_copy` copies exactly (lengths 0..2048+, past the 512-byte threshold) | `fastcpy::tests::test_fast_short_slice_copy` | Port of existing proptest (which only used default small vec sizes) |
+| 13 | `fastcpy_unsafe::slice_copy` copies exactly (lengths 1..2048+; empty is outside its domain, as in the original test) | `fastcpy_unsafe::tests::test_fast_short_slice_copy` | Port of existing proptest; only compiled without the safe-encode/safe-decode features |
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test` (default features): lib unit tests **19 passed / 0 failed** (includes port #12),
+doc tests 10 passed, `tests/tests.rs` **52 passed / 2 failed / 1 ignored** (the pre-existing
+`#[ignore]` on `big_compression`).
+
+| Test | Result |
+|------|--------|
+| hegel_roundtrip_block_and_frame | PASS |
+| hegel_prepend_size_consistency | PASS |
+| hegel_roundtrip_with_dict | PASS |
+| hegel_compress_into_max_output_size_is_sufficient | PASS |
+| hegel_compress_table_reuse_roundtrips | PASS |
+| hegel_decompress_accepts_larger_size_hint | PASS |
+| frame::hegel_frame_roundtrip_any_config | **FAIL — suspected bug 1** |
+| frame::hegel_frame_write_chunking_invariance | PASS |
+| frame::hegel_frame_read_chunking_invariance | PASS |
+| frame::hegel_frame_concatenated_frames | **FAIL — suspected bug 2** |
+| frame::hegel_frame_corruption_never_panics_or_misdecodes | PASS |
+| fastcpy::tests::test_fast_short_slice_copy | PASS |
+| fastcpy_unsafe::tests::test_fast_short_slice_copy | PASS (run with `--no-default-features --features std,frame,checked-decode`) |
+
+Verification steps per the skill: liveness confirmed by temporarily inverting an
+assertion in `hegel_prepend_size_consistency` (hegel reported a shrunk counterexample,
+then reverted); one exploratory run at `test_cases = 1000` (10x) found nothing beyond
+the two failures below; the whole suite was additionally run with
+`--no-default-features --features std,frame,checked-decode` (unsafe encode/decode
+paths) with identical results — same 2 failures, everything else passes.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### Bug 1: `BlockSize::Max8MB` produces frames that no decoder accepts
+
+Minimal counterexample (shrunk by hegel):
+
+```text
+data = []
+frame_info = FrameInfo { block_size: Max8MB, block_mode: Independent, ..default }
+→ FrameEncoder writes the frame fine, FrameDecoder fails with Error::ReservedBitsSet
+```
+
+Why it's a library bug: `BlockSize::Max8MB` is a public variant accepted by
+`FrameInfo::block_size()`, and the encoder happily emits a frame — but the emitted
+header is invalid. Code path: `FrameInfo::write` in `src/frame/header.rs` line 250 does
+`buffer[5] = (self.block_size as u8) << BD_BLOCK_SIZE_MASK_RSHIFT`. `Max8MB = 8`, so
+`8 << 4 = 0x80`: this sets the reserved bit 7 of the BD byte and leaves the block-size
+bits at `0`. Per the LZ4 frame spec (and `FrameInfo::read`, which only accepts size
+codes 4..=7 and rejects any reserved bit), such a frame is malformed — the C++ lz4
+would reject it too. `Max8MB` exists for the *legacy* frame format (which the decoder
+supports for reading), but the encoder never writes legacy frames, so encoding with
+`Max8MB` can never be valid. `FrameInfo::write` (or the encoder) should reject or clamp
+it instead of emitting a corrupt header. The test is kept failing as written.
+
+### Bug 2: `try_finish` after a finished frame with no new data panics (debug) / writes a stray EndMark (release)
+
+Minimal counterexample (shrunk by hegel):
+
+```text
+payloads = [[], []]   // also fails for [data, []]
+for p in payloads { write_all(p); try_finish(); }
+→ second try_finish panics: "assertion failed: self.is_frame_open",
+  src/frame/compress.rs:210 (debug_assert in end_frame)
+```
+
+Why it's a library bug: `try_finish` is documented to "finish this output stream,
+flushing internal buffer and writing stream terminator", and it contains an explicit
+empty-input special case that writes a valid empty frame. But that special case in
+`FrameEncoder::try_finish` (`src/frame/compress.rs` lines 173–187) is gated on
+`!self.data_to_frame_written` — i.e. "no frame has *ever* been finished by this
+encoder" — instead of "no data since the *last* finished frame". So the first
+`try_finish` with no data works (writes an empty frame), but any later `try_finish`
+with no intervening writes skips `begin_frame` and calls `end_frame()` with no open
+frame: in debug builds the `debug_assert!(self.is_frame_open)` at line 210 panics; in
+release builds it silently writes a spurious EndMark (and content checksum, if
+enabled) into the output stream — bytes that are not part of any frame. The fix would
+be to track "data written since last finished frame" (or make the guard
+`!self.is_frame_open && nothing_buffered → begin_frame`) so every `try_finish`
+produces a valid (possibly empty) frame. The test is kept failing as written.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Byte-equality between `compress_into_with_table` and `compress_into` across table
+  reuse.** Abandoned during design (before running): `HashTable4KU16` and `HashTable4K`
+  use *different hash functions* (`hash` vs `hash5` in `src/block/hashtable.rs`), and the
+  documented Small→Large upgrade is one-way — after a >=64KB input, subsequent small
+  inputs are compressed with the Large table and legitimately produce different (still
+  valid) bytes than `compress_into` would. Replaced with the sound per-input roundtrip
+  property (#5).
+- **Byte-equality between chunked writes and a single `write_all` in the frame
+  encoder.** Abandoned during design: intermediate `flush`es change block boundaries, and
+  `BlockSize::Auto` is resolved from the *first* write's length (`begin_frame`), so byte
+  identity is not promised anywhere. Weakened to the documented contract: both decompress
+  to the same data (#8).
+- **`Max8MB` in the generators of the other frame properties (#8, #9).** Not unsound,
+  but excluded there with a source comment so that those properties don't re-fail on the
+  already-covered Bug 1; `hegel_frame_roundtrip_any_config` keeps generating it.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked well — the two real bugs were found by exactly the patterns it
+recommends ("generate configurations, not just payloads" found Bug 1; broad generators
+that don't exclude empty inputs found Bug 2). Specific notes:
+
+1. **The MSRV note in `references/rust/reference.md` was load-bearing and accurate.**
+   `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed against the crate's `rust-version = "1.81"`
+   (hegeltest requires 1.86); the documented `--ignore-rust-version` retry worked
+   first time. Without that note this would have been the most likely
+   abandon-hegel moment.
+2. **The 10x exploratory run is more manual than it needs to be.** Gotcha 13 says
+   there is no global case-count override, so raising 12 tests to `test_cases = 1000`
+   and reverting meant sed-editing attributes in three files. A supported env-var
+   override (or a documented pattern, e.g. a shared `const` if attribute arguments
+   accept one) would make this step much less error-prone. This is the biggest
+   workflow gap I hit.
+3. **The "keep the failing test as written" rule could say what to do with sibling
+   properties.** Bug 1 would have made three otherwise-passing frame properties fail
+   for the same known reason. I excluded the buggy input from the *other* tests with
+   comments pointing at the failing test, keeping one canonical failure. The skill
+   never says whether this is sanctioned; a sentence blessing (or forbidding) this
+   pattern would remove the judgment call.
+4. **Failure output naming worked nicely** — draws bound to variables print as
+   `let data = []` / `let frame_info = ...`, which made the shrunk counterexamples
+   directly readable. Inline draws (e.g. inside `if tc.draw(...)`) print as `draw_1`;
+   the reference could mention that binding draws to named variables improves
+   counterexample readability.
+5. **Composites with parameters worked exactly as documented**
+   (`frame_config(include_max_8mb: bool)`), as did `one_of!`, `sampled_from` over an
+   owned `Vec` of enum values, and stacking `#[cfg_attr(miri, ignore)]` under
+   `#[hegel::test]` (compiles; not verified under miri).
+6. **porting.md's checklist was actionable.** Enumerating the old suite first (3
+   proptest properties: one integration roundtrip, two `fastcpy` copies) made it easy
+   to guarantee superset coverage and to know when removing the `proptest`
+   dev-dependency was safe. The "broaden the generators" instruction directly
+   improved the fastcpy ports (old tests never exceeded proptest's default vec sizes;
+   the copy routine branches at 4/8/16/32/64/512 bytes).
+7. Minor: the skill's liveness check leaves a `.hegel/` failure-database entry, and
+   gotcha 3/11 correctly predicted both the directory's appearance and the cleanup;
+   no surprises.
+
+Environment note (not a skill issue, disclosed for transparency): the shared build
+disk filled up mid-build; I deleted two *build-artifact* directories of sibling
+investigation targets (`targets/fjall-fable/target`, `targets/sqlparser-fable/target`)
+to free space. No source files outside this crate were touched.
